 r"I am writing to apply for the Product Analyst position at Accurx. I am completing an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, where a distinction is expected, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a Data Analyst Intern at TikTok Shop UK. Across my academic and professional experience I have worked on defining what to measure, running experiments to test whether a change actually helped, and turning the result into a recommendation someone could act on. I am particularly interested in this role because it combines product analytics with healthcare, which are the two areas I have spent the last two years in.",

 r"What attracts me most to Accurx is that the analysis has a visible end point: clinician time saved, and patients who can book or be triaged without friction. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved. The posting describes hunting for insights and challenging assumptions rather than running reports, and that is the part of analytics I want to do. My MSc has also given me a working understanding of how NHS primary care data is structured and why it is often messy, which I expect matters when interpreting what the numbers are telling you.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I mine merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python to find the growth and operations drivers behind the UK marketplace, and I build the KPI frameworks and dashboards that commercial and operations teams use to set targets and track initiatives. Working with product, operations and commercial stakeholders is the normal shape of my week, and the recurring lesson has been that the first metric someone asks for is rarely the one that answers their question. I also built an AI agent that automates repetitive steps of my own analytics workflow, which my team and partner teams now use; the difficult part was deciding which steps it should own and then supporting colleagues through the change.",

 r"At DS Digital Technology I worked as a Data Scientist Intern on a medical AI engagement. I designed a 246-question benchmark and a six-dimension rubric with clinical experts, which the client adopted as their acceptance standard, and I built a Python pipeline calling six LLM APIs that produced and scored over 2,400 responses. I then ran blinded paired A/B tests to measure whether a medically reviewed knowledge base improved answer quality, and reported which differences the data supported and which it did not. Designing the measure before running the test, and being willing to report a null result, is the habit I would bring to experimentation at Accurx.",

 r"My MSc dissertation applies predictive and causal machine learning to 458,840 UK Biobank participants, estimating the effect of lifestyle changes on cardiovascular risk using doubly robust causal forests, with sensitivity analyses for unmeasured confounding and external validation on a second cohort. It is delivered as a deployed application so that others can run scenarios without touching the code. That project is where I learned to separate a correlation that looks useful from an effect that would survive being acted on.",

 r"At Ipsos Strategy3 I structured and analysed data from 80 or more healthcare professional interviews, built market-sizing and forecasting models, and turned the analysis into dashboards and recommendations for client stakeholders. That is where I learned to present a finding to an audience who wants the implication rather than the method.",

 r"I should be clear about one gap. I have not used Mixpanel or Amplitude, and I have not yet owned product metrics like activation, retention and cohort behaviour under those names. My experimentation, SQL and dashboarding foundations are solid and I have picked up new tooling quickly before, but I would be learning the product analytics stack in my first weeks rather than arriving with it.",

 r"I would bring Accurx strong SQL and Python, genuine experience designing and interpreting experiments, healthcare data knowledge from a specialist MSc, and the habit of checking whether what I built actually worked before recommending it. I have the right to work in the UK until January 2029 without sponsorship. I am based in London and can work from the Shoreditch office three days a week.",

